 To implement the bubble sort algorithm in C++ with minimal memory usage and potential errors, you can follow these steps:
1. Define a function that takes the array as a pointer and the size of the array as arguments. This will minimize memory usage by not creating a copy of the array.
2. Use two pointers, one representing the current position and the other representing the next position, to iterate through the array.
3. Inside the loop, compare the elements at the current and next positions and swap them if they are in the wrong order.
4. Repeat step 3 until no swaps are made, indicating that the array is sorted.
Here's an example implementation of the bubble sort algorithm in C++:
```c++
#include <iostream>
void bubbleSort(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                int temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
            }
        }
    }
}
int main() {
    int arr[] = {5, 2, 9, 1, 5, 6};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    bubbleSort(arr, n);
    // Print sorted array
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        std::cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    return 0;
}
```
