 To call a PowerShell script that requires user input (Read-Host) from Terraform, you cannot directly use Terraform's built-in functions to execute the script and handle the user input. However, there are workarounds using external methods. Here's an approach using a PowerShell Core executable with environment variables:

1. Create a PowerShell script named `example.ps1` without any user inputs:

```powershell
param(
    [Parameter(Mandatory=$true)]
    [string]$InputParam
)

Write-Output "Input parameter value: $InputParam"
```

2. Pass the required input as an environment variable when calling the PowerShell script from Terraform:

```hcl
provider "local" {}

locals {
  input_value = "My Input Value"
}

resource "local_exec" "powershell_script" {
  command = jsonencode({
    command = "powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypassthrough -File C:/path/to/your/example.ps1 -InputParam \"${local.input_value}\""
  })

  environment = {
    "PS1_NON_INTERACTIVE" = "1"
  }
}
```

Replace `C:/path/to/your/example.ps1` with the actual path to your PowerShell script. Make sure PowerShell Core is installed on your system where Terraform runs.

This method sets the `PS1_NON_INTERACTIVE` environment variable to '1' to prevent PowerShell from showing any interactive prompts. Instead, it will take the provided input from the script parameters.

Remember, this method bypasses PowerShell's execution policy for security reasons. Use it only in trusted environments.

