 To check if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric in R, you can use the `sapply()` function with the `as.numeric()` and `is.na()` functions. Here's an example of how to write the R code:

```R
# Define character vector with NA values and some strings that cannot be converted to numeric
char_vector <- c("3", "NA", "1.5", "five", "7", NA)

# Function to check if each element can be converted to numeric
can_convert <- function(x) {
  result <- tryCatch(as.numeric(x), error = function(e) NA, warning = function(w) NA)
  is.na(result) == FALSE
}

# Use sapply to apply the can_convert function to each non-NA element
numeric_elements <- sapply(char_vector[!is.na(char_vector)], can_convert)

# Check if all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric
if (all(numeric_elements)) {
  cat("All non-NA elements in the character vector can be converted to numeric.\n")
} else {
  cat("Some non-NA elements in the character vector cannot be converted to numeric.\n")
}
```

In this example, the `can_convert()` function attempts to convert each character value to numeric using `as.numeric()`, and returns `TRUE` if the conversion is successful. If an error or warning occurs during the conversion, the function returns `NA`. The `sapply()` function applies the `can_convert()` function to each non-NA element in the character vector, and the resulting logical vector indicates whether each element can be converted to numeric. Finally, we use `all()` to check if all elements in the logical vector are `TRUE`.

