 Generating a haiku using C# code about a lone tree standing tall in the midst of a misty meadow is an interesting challenge! However, it's important to note that creating poetry with code is subjective and may not produce perfect results every time. Here's a simple example of how you might structure your C# code and HTML display for this scenario:

First, let's create some predefined phrases for our haiku. These phrases will capture the essence of the scene we want to describe:

```csharp
using System;

namespace HaikuGenerator
{
    class Program
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            string tree = "Lone tree stands tall";
            string mist = "Mist veils morning light";
            string meadow = "In quiet meadow's heart";

            GenerateHaiku(tree, mist, meadow);
        }

        static void GenerateHaiku(string tree, string mist, string meadow)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{tree} \n{mist}\n{meadow}");
            CreateHtmlFile(tree, mist, meadow);
        }

        static void CreateHtmlFile(string tree, string mist, string meadow)
        {
            string html = $@"<html>
                                <head>
                                    <title>Haiku</title>
                                </head>
                                <body>
                                    <h1>Haiku</h1>
                                    <p>{tree}</p>
                                    <p>{mist}</p>
                                    <p>{meadow}</p>
                                </body>
                            </html>";

            File.WriteAllText("haiku.html", html);
            Console.WriteLine("HTML file created successfully.");
        }
    }
}
```

This code creates three strings containing phrases that fit into the 5-7-5 syllable pattern of a traditional haiku. It then generates the haiku by printing it to the console and writing it to an HTML file named `haiku.html`.

